D to Safe D", "Prediction markets price Powell around 92 percent to win", "Powell's $2.7M raised plus $3.1M in outside support dwarfs Harding's $2M with no outside backing", "Only one poll (D+4, n=182, mid-September) and an open seat leave the margin uncertain"], "rationale": "Bacon's retirement hands Democrats a D+3 open seat where Powell outraised Harding and holds a massive outside-spending edge; every major rater and the betting markets line up behind a flip, and the lone public poll points the same way. Harding's local roots and the October debate could keep it closer, but the weight of evidence favors Powell by high single digits.", "watch": "A strong Harding debate performance on October 14 or a late wave of Republican outside money could tighten the race if new polling shows movement.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Don Bacon (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Don Bacon's retirement opens an Omaha-area district that leans three points Democratic, and both parties treat it as a top flip opportunity. Denise Powell holds the financial edge, raising $2.7 million and drawing about $3.1 million in outside support, while Brinker Harding has raised $2 million with essentially no outside help. Powell has collected the Omaha mayor's endorsement and a unifying op-ed from a former primary rival; Harding has campaigned with national Republicans and pitched affordability fixes.\n\nRaters across the spectrum place the seat in Democratic hands, ranging from Tilt D to Safe D, and prediction markets give Powell roughly a 92 percent chance. The only public poll, from mid-September, showed Powell up four among likely voters, though its small sample limits precision. With an open seat and thin polling, some narrowing is plausible, but the weight of evidence points to a Democratic pickup.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9278, "margin": 8.8}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9035, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.914, "marg